---
import PluginDocsLayout from '../../layouts/PluginDocsLayout.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';

const tierSnippet = `class HelloPlugin : public agent::IPlugin {
public:
    std::string id()      const override { return "hello"; }
    std::string version() const override { return "1.0.0"; }
    std::string name()    const override { return "Hello"; }

    bool initialize(const agent::PluginContext&) override { return true; }
    void shutdown() override {}

    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<agent::Capability>> capabilities() override;
};`;

const capSnippet = `std::vector<std::unique_ptr<agent::Capability>> HelloPlugin::capabilities() {
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<agent::Capability>> caps;
    caps.push_back(std::make_unique<agent::ToolCapability>(
        "greet", [](agent::PluginServices&) {
            std::vector<std::unique_ptr<agent::Tool>> tools;
            tools.push_back(std::make_unique<GreetTool>());
            return tools;   // empty list = declined, not an error
        }));
    return caps;
}`;

const sourceDir = `${site.repo}/tree/main/docs/spec/plugins`;
const repo = site.repo;

const cmdSnippet = `agent::CommandSpec spec;
spec.root = "hello";
spec.help = "Say hello";
spec.subtree = agent::json::parse(R"({"greet": {"help": "Greet someone"}})");
spec.handlers["greet"] = [](const std::string& arg) {
    return "Hello, " + (arg.empty() ? std::string("world") : arg) + "!";
};
caps.push_back(std::make_unique<agent::CommandCapability>(std::move(spec)));`;
---

<PluginDocsLayout
  title="Overview"
  description="How to extend amber: core plugins in C++17 and external plugins in any language. Reference, examples and quickstart."
  current="index"
>
  <h1>Plugin docs</h1>

  <p>
    Amber is built to be extended. Its own providers, core tools, status bar and
    registry console are all plugins, the same API described here. There is no
    privileged internal path.
  </p>

  <h2>Two tiers</h2>

  <p>
    Where a plugin runs is an isolation decision, not a size decision. Pick
    deliberately:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>Core plugin</th><th>External plugin</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Language</td><td>C++17, compiled in</td><td>Any executable</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Isolation</td><td>Same process; a crash takes amber down</td><td>Separate process; a crash cannot</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Can contribute</td><td>Tools, providers, prompt blocks, status segments, panels, wallets, allowances, event subscriptions</td><td>Tools only</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Distribution</td><td><code>plugins/&lt;id&gt;/</code>, compiled in</td><td><code>~/.config/amber/plugins/&lt;id&gt;/</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost per call</td><td>none (direct call)</td><td>IPC round trip</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Which tier?</h2>
  <Diagram
    name="pluginTierChoice"
  />

  <h2>Quickstart</h2>

  <p>
    A complete, compiled, tested example ships in the repository at
    <code>examples/plugin_hello/</code> (exercised by
    <code>tests/example_plugin_test.cpp</code>). The shape:
  </p>

  <div class="terminal"><pre>{tierSnippet}</pre></div>

  <p>Then declare what it contributes:</p>

  <div class="terminal"><pre>{capSnippet}</pre></div>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>Create <code>plugins/&lt;id&gt;/</code> with your plugin class.</li>
    <li>Declare capabilities in <code>capabilities()</code>.</li>
    <li>Register it in <code>make_bundled_plugins()</code> (<code>lib/plugins_bundled.cpp</code>) and add the object to <code>Makefile.in</code>.</li>
    <li>Write a hermetic test (see <a href="/plugins/testing">Testing</a>).</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    <strong>You declare; the runtime installs.</strong> Every contribution goes
    through the ledger, which is what makes disable total. A plugin that
    registers outside this path cannot be disabled cleanly, that is the one
    rule the framework is built around.
  </p>

  <h3>Slash command quickstart</h3>
  <p>
    To expose logic through the slash engine, contribute a
    <code>CommandCapability</code>: declare the JSON mapping, expose the callback,
    and the host binds it. The bundled <code>plugins/hello/</code> is this exact
    example, type <code>/hello greet amber</code> and it returns
    <code>Hello, amber!</code>
  </p>
  <div class="terminal"><pre>{cmdSnippet}</pre></div>
  <p>
    The handler returns the text to display and the host prints it, so the plugin
    stays free of any UI dependency. See
    <a href="/plugins/capabilities">Capabilities → Command</a> for the full
    contract.
  </p>

  <h2>In this reference</h2>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li><a href="/plugins/anatomy">Plugin anatomy</a>, <code>IPlugin</code>, <code>PluginContext</code>, <code>PluginServices</code>, the ledger, lifecycle</li>
    <li><a href="/plugins/capabilities">Capabilities</a>, every capability kind, with code</li>
    <li><a href="/plugins/events">Events</a>, the typed event bus, subscribe and intercept, full catalogue</li>
    <li><a href="/plugins/providers">Providers</a>, add an LLM provider, with wallet and allowance</li>
    <li><a href="/plugins/external">External plugins</a>, the JSON-RPC protocol and manifest</li>
    <li><a href="/plugins/testing">Testing &amp; review</a>, hermetic tests and the PR checklist</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Availability</h2>
  <p>
    What ships today, at a glance. The
    <a href={`${sourceDir}/developer-guide.md`} rel="noopener">developer guide</a>
    is the canonical source; this table mirrors it.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>External tool plugin (JSON-RPC)</td><td>✅ Available</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tool contribution (incl. core tools)</td><td>✅ Available</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Typed event subscription</td><td>✅ Available</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Prompt block contribution</td><td>✅ Available</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Provider contribution</td><td>✅ Available</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Status segment contribution</td><td>✅ Available</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Panel contribution</td><td>✅ Available</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wallet / allowance readout</td><td>✅ Available</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Command contribution (slash namespace)</td><td>✅ Available</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Host services to the user (ask/choose/confirm)</td><td>Not available</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Per-plugin settings, log sinks</td><td>Not available</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>PluginLoaded</code> / <code>PluginUnloaded</code> events</td><td>Declared, never published</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Source</h2>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li><a href={repo} rel="noopener">Repository</a></li>
    <li><a href={sourceDir} rel="noopener">docs/spec/plugins/</a>, design record, developer guide, external protocol</li>
    <li><a href={`${repo}/blob/main/lib/plugins_bundled.cpp`} rel="noopener">lib/plugins_bundled.cpp</a>, the shipped set</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Found a gap in the framework? That is a
    <a href="/report">bug report or feature request</a>, and a good one.
  </p>
</PluginDocsLayout>

